    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "I think the rhetoric is hollow and I don't see this both capital, both political and real and financial capital. I don't see the majorities in the Senate and the House want to expend that. And it has become a hollow win for the president. More and more difficulties, not only from suits on eminent domain, the tribe in my district saying, on these 90 miles, you're not going to do it. We have to go to court, we'll do that. And the lawsuit that we filed, along with the Center for Biological Diversity is, again, talking about the fact that one, it's not needed, and two, cost in terms of money, yes, but the overriding cost of what it's going to do not only to the area itself environmentally, but to a variety of species that are part of that Sonoran Desert. We feel that it's going to have a hard time. And that's the beginning of the pushback on that wall. It hasn't really intensified. And I don't see too many Republicans wanting to put all their political and financial capital on that wall.",
