  {
    "episode_id": 94534,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94534.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Well, the first thing is the unity that on the tough votes, if we lose one or two or three, that's minimal compared to other situations. So in pushing back and voting against Trump initiatives that are in the legislative arena, I'm very happy. And I think it's very gratifying for the public in general and certainly for our base to see our party standing together and being unified. That's number one. I think that has both surprised me and it's very gratifying. The other area in terms of the kind of activity that is occurring all across this nation with the almost organic development of groups and individuals across this country that are involved in issues, I know I can speak for myself in my district, I'm working with groups and individuals that I have not seen participating in a campaign or in political activity in the past, but they are engaged and very much committed to it. And I think the smart thing that sometimes members have individually made a mistake of trying to grab the flag and running in front of them. I think this is a time in our... that's going on right now. We need to be partners. We need to be allies and where we can be very effective, whether in my case, a lawsuit or the profile and give voice to a particular issue, we should. But I think that us organically, some of these organizations that are developing, not to take the flag and try to run so far ahead that we forget that there is a movement out there that we need to be, we need to complement, not try to direct. I hope we don't make the mistake of trying to direct this energy.",
